Project Use Cases
    - Creation of New Trails
        Robot explores untapped forest areas to identify and log potential walkable paths.

    - Mapping of Existing Trails
        Generates accurate GPS/SLAM-based maps of current trails for navigation apps and park management.

    - Enhanced Trail Visualization
        Future integration with a 3D viewer for immersive path exploration and planning.

    - Trail Condition Assessment (added)
        Monitors erosion, vegetation growth, and obstacle presence for maintenance planning.



Challenges & Limitations
    - Large Elevation Steps
        Boulders, large rocks, or fallen logs may exceed the robot’s climbing ability.

    - Dense Vegetation
        High vegetation in many Australian forests could block progress unless the path is pre-cleared.

    - Unexpected Obstacles
        Robot can be stopped by unforeseen blockages if no prior path preparation is done.

    - Water Crossings
        Rivers or creeks require the robot to be fully waterproof or equipped with amphibious capabilities.

    - Man-made Structures
        Stairs or ladders on established trails may not be traversable by most ground robots.

    - Cost vs. Human Labor
        Could be more expensive than sending people who can also clear and maintain trails during mapping.

    - Navigation Under Canopy (added)
        GPS signal loss under dense tree cover makes reliable navigation harder without advanced SLAM.



Potential Robot Platforms
    - Quadruped Robot (Dog-like)
        Example: Boston Dynamics Spot.
        Pros: High mobility, can step over obstacles, adaptable to uneven terrain.
        Cons: Expensive, limited battery life, payload constraints.

    - Wheeled Rover with Large Wheels
        Pros: Simple design, efficient on moderately rough terrain, cost-effective.
        Cons: Struggles with very large obstacles and deep mud.

    - Tracked Robot (Tank-like)
        Pros: Excellent traction, stable on slopes, good in mud/snow.
        Cons: Heavier, higher power consumption, less efficient on smooth paths.

    - Hybrid Platforms (added)
        Combination of wheels + tracks or leg-wheel hybrids for maximum adaptability.
        


1) Definition of a Valid Trail (Walkable Trail)

A trail is considered valid if it meets the following criteria:
    Height Clearance:
        - Minimum 2 m clearance above the trail.
        - Possible detection: upward-facing radar or LiDAR.

    Trail Width:
        - Minimum 1 m for comfortable walking.
        - Detection: RGB-D camera or LiDAR-based width estimation.

    Slope & Elevation Changes:
        - Preferred slope: −15° to +15° incline.
        - Short sections (max 5 m) can reach up to 24°.
        - Detection: 6-axis IMU for pitch/roll measurement.

    Surface Type:
        - Should be walkable (e.g., compact soil, grass, rock).
        - Avoid long stretches of mud, water, or unstable rock.
        - Detection: RGB/RGB-D camera with AI model for surface classification (optional for MVP).

    Position & Mapping:
        - GPS (or GNSS + SLAM) for path recording and mapping.
        - Generate trail map for later review.

    Distance & Travel Time:
        - Use odometry to measure precise trail length.
        - Combine with speed to estimate travel time.

    Step Height Limit:
        - Define maximum vertical obstacle height robot can traverse (TBD based on platform).
        - Detection: LiDAR or depth camera height profile analysis.
        


2) Drone-Assisted Trail Finding

    Purpose:
        - Drone flies ahead to scout trails and obstacles.
        - Sends processed data to ground trail robot for navigation assistance.

    Detection:
        - Use RGB camera for aerial trail detection.
        - Identify potential obstacles ahead.

    Benefits:
        - Faster navigation.
        - Reduced risk of dead ends or impassable obstacles.
        


3) Test Environment Requirements

General Layout:
    Dense forest environment with:
        - Many trees and bushes.
        - Fallen branches and rocks.
        - Natural dead ends and false trails.

Terrain Features:
    - Varying elevation (hills, valleys, slopes).
    - Multiple elevation levels to simulate real-world forest.
    - Both easy and difficult trail options.

Trail Network:
    - Length: 1–3 km total.
    - Segments: 50–150 m each.
    - Intersections: 1–3 per map.
    - Obstructions: 2–5 per trail (fallen branches, shallow water, rock steps).

Difficulty Variation:
    - Easy trails: low slope, wide, few obstacles.
    - Hard trails: higher slope, narrower, more obstacles.

Generation Approach:
    Consider procedural generation with rule-based distribution:
        - Tree density ranges.
        - Vegetation and bush distribution.
        - Randomized rock placement.
        - Streams or shallow water crossings.

    Controlled randomness for repeatable tests (seeded generator).
    

